aarch64: keep guest mmap allocations below 2^47 for Darwin's 47-bit pointers.

Darwin userspace pointers are 47-bit -- objc FAST_DATA_MASK (0x7ffffffffff8), tagged pointers and
isa masking all assume bit 47 is clear. The aarch64 Linux mmap area is 48-bit and the kernel places
a non-fixed mapping topdown (bit 47 set) unless a hint lands it low. libmalloc reserves its zone
regions via mach_vm_allocate(ANYWHERE) with a bogus minimum-address hint (0x1000) that the kernel
ignores, so the region -- and every heap object in it, e.g. objc class_rw -- ends up above 2^47 and
FAST_DATA_MASK corrupts it (readClass/realizeClass faults). Force every non-MAP_FIXED mapping into a
low bump arena (dyld and libSystem get disjoint arenas via VARIANT_DYLD); MAP_FIXED (an exact
address the guest derived from a prior low reservation) is honoured. x86_64 is unaffected.
--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/mman/mman.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/mman/mman.c
@@ -40,6 +40,36 @@
 	// if (flags & MAP_STACK)
 	// 	linux_flags |= LINUX_MAP_STACK;
 
+#if defined(__aarch64__) || defined(__arm64__)
+	// aarch64 port: Darwin userspace pointers are 47-bit -- objc's FAST_DATA_MASK (0x7ffffffffff8),
+	// tagged pointers and isa masking all assume bit 47 is clear. The Linux aarch64 mmap area hands
+	// back 48-bit addresses with bit 47 SET, so a class-data pointer masked by objc becomes an
+	// unmapped address and readClass() faults. For a kernel-chosen mapping (start == 0, no
+	// MAP_FIXED), hand the kernel a low hint from a monotonically advancing arena so guest dylibs
+	// and heap land below 2^47; Linux honours a free hint even under topdown allocation. MAP_FIXED
+	// requests (addresses the guest derived from these low reservations) pass through untouched.
+	// dyld and the shared libSystem link separate copies of this handler, so give them disjoint
+	// arenas (VARIANT_DYLD) to keep their bump pointers from colliding.
+	// Force EVERY non-MAP_FIXED mapping into a low arena (below 2^47). On aarch64 Linux the mmap
+	// area is 48-bit and the kernel places a non-fixed mapping topdown (bit 47 set) unless the hint
+	// lands it low -- and libmalloc hints its zone regions with a bogus minimum-address value
+	// (0x1000, VM_FLAGS_ANYWHERE) that the kernel ignores, so the region (and every heap object in
+	// it: objc class_rw et al.) ends up above 2^47 where Darwin's 47-bit FAST_DATA_MASK corrupts it.
+	// A Darwin guest never legitimately wants a bit-47-set address and a non-fixed hint carries no
+	// guarantee, so we override the hint outright. MAP_FIXED (an exact address the guest derived
+	// from a prior low reservation) is always honoured.
+	if (!(flags & MAP_FIXED))
+	{
+#ifdef VARIANT_DYLD
+		static unsigned long mmap_low_next = 0x0000100000000000UL; // 16 TiB (dyld)
+#else
+		static unsigned long mmap_low_next = 0x0000400000000000UL; // 64 TiB (libSystem)
+#endif
+		unsigned long rounded = (len + 0x1fffffUL) & ~0x1fffffUL; // round up to 2 MiB
+		start = (void*) __atomic_fetch_add(&mmap_low_next, rounded, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+	}
+#endif
+
 #ifdef __NR_mmap2
 #ifdef __i386__
 	linux_flags |= LINUX_MAP_32BIT;
